---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";

import ConceptFigure from "@/components/temporal-agent-orchestration/ConceptFigure.astro";
import TermTip from "@/components/temporal-agent-orchestration/TermTip.astro";
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";

import "asciinema-player/dist/bundle/asciinema-player.css";

const root = "/temporal-agent-orchestration";
const cast = `${root}/demo/worker-kill.cast`;
const beforeCast = `${root}/demo/before-temporal.cast`;
const evidence = `${root}/demo/artifacts`;

// Read at build time rather than transcribed: a hand-copied revision goes stale
// the first time the demo is re-recorded, and a stale one is worse than none.
const provenance = JSON.parse(
  readFileSync(
    path.join(
      process.cwd(),
      "public/temporal-agent-orchestration/demo/artifacts/provenance.json",
    ),
    "utf8",
  ),
) as {
  service_revision_full: string;
  published_files_sha256: Record<string, string>;
};
---

<PageLayout
  title="How I made Gas City's agent orchestration durable with Temporal"
  kicker="Temporal case study"
  intro="An agent in a software factory is editing code when the orchestration process crashes before recording the handoff. The task still exists, but the steps needed to resume it are lost. Temporal makes those steps durable without requiring the agent itself to be deterministic."
  description="A short companion to the full Gas City case study: what I Temporalized, what the Worker-kill demo proves, and where the guarantees stop."
  noindex={true}
>
  <article class="tco">
    <section class="tco__section tco__summary">
      <blockquote class="tco__lede">
        <p>
          Put the unpredictable agent inside an Activity. Put the promises
          around it in a Workflow.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <dl class="tco__ledger">
        <div>
          <dt>Temporalized</dt>
          <dd>One ready task → one agent session → one fenced receipt.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Proved</dt>
          <dd>Recovery from a real Worker kill without starting a second agent.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Running today</dt>
          <dd>Durable result delivery and acknowledgement.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Not proved</dt>
          <dd>Cross-host recovery.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <nav class="tco__tabs" aria-label="Case study materials">
        <a href="#worker-kill"><span>01</span> Watch the Worker-kill demo</a>
        <a href={`${root}/article`}><span>02</span> Read the full article</a>
        <a href={`${root}/code`}><span>03</span> Inspect the annotated code</a>
      </nav>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">The problem</p>
        <h2>A crash loses the procedure, not the task</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          I run a system that hands tracked work to coding agents. Work items
          live in a durable store, <TermTip
            term="beads"
            tip="The durable store of work facts: tasks, claims, artifacts, and receipts. It is why a crash never loses the task itself; what a crash used to lose was everything in flight around the task."
          >Beads</TermTip>, so a crash never loses the task. A crash used to
          lose everything around the task. Three windows defined the problem:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The process dies after claiming the work but before launching the agent.</li>
          <li>The process dies after launching the agent but before recording the session.</li>
          <li>An old attempt finishes late and overwrites the current result.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Similar gaps existed between completion, delivery, and exact
          acknowledgement.
        </p>
        <p>
          The task state was already durable. What was missing was a durable
          owner for the procedure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section tco__numbered">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Section 01</p>
        <h2>What I Temporalized</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          I made one procedure durable: assign one ready work item to one agent
          session and accept a result only from the current attempt.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <TermTip
            term="workflow"
            tip="Temporal's deterministic procedure code. Every step is recorded in an Event History, so a replacement process can replay the history and continue from the recorded boundary."
          >Workflow</TermTip> owns the order: claim, launch, wait, and receive.
          An <TermTip
            term="activity"
            tip="Where Temporal runs nondeterministic and external work. Activities execute at least once, so retries must be made safe by the application."
          >Activity</TermTip> performs the nondeterministic work: updating the
          store, starting or finding the agent session, heartbeating, and
          reporting completion.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <TermTip
            term="generation-fence"
            tip="A work item's generation is its attempt number: retry the work and the generation goes up. The fence is the guard that makes staleness harmless, because a receipt only lands if it carries the current generation."
          >generation fence</TermTip> prevents an older attempt from
          overwriting the current one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <table class="tco__table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Before</th>
            <th scope="col">After</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A process-owned reconcile tick</td>
            <td>A durable Workflow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Recovery inferred from stored facts</td>
            <td>Recovery replays recorded history</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Launch identity was reconstructive</td>
            <td>Launch identity is stable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Late results could overwrite current work</td>
            <td>Generation-fenced receipts</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <ul>
          <li>Beads owns work facts.</li>
          <li>Temporal owns procedural progress.</li>
          <li>An Activity is where Temporal hands off to the external world.</li>
          <li>The application owns what that Activity touches: identity, fencing, idempotency, and correctness.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <ConceptFigure kind="ownership" />
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          The exact type and queue names, and every file at its pinned
          revision, live in <a href={`${root}/code`}>the annotated code</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section tco__numbered" id="worker-kill">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Section 02</p>
        <h2>The Worker dies on camera</h2>
        <p>
          Two recordings, three demonstrated executions: the before state
          failing once, then Temporal surviving the same kill twice, once
          after a heartbeat checkpoint and once before any checkpoint exists.
          Both recordings are controlled runs of the same fixture; only the
          orchestrator changes. Each is a split screen: the run on the left,
          and on the right independent panes that measure for themselves
          rather than trusting the run's own narration.
        </p>
      </header>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          <strong>I kill the orchestration process, not the coding agent.</strong>
          The agent continues working after its coordinator disappears. The
          question is whether the restarted orchestration attaches to that
          same agent or launches a competitor.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="tco__provenance">
        Source for these runs:
        <a href="https://github.com/sjarmak/gas-city">sjarmak/gas-city</a>; the
        <a href={`${evidence}/provenance.json`}>provenance record</a> names the
        revision, SDK, server, and host, and hashes each of the
        {" "}{Object.keys(provenance.published_files_sha256).length} published
        evidence files.
      </p>
      <h3 class="tco__demo-subhead">First, without Temporal</h3>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          In the before state, the orchestrator was the reconcile loop. It
          claimed the work, launched the agent, and kept the remaining
          procedure only in process memory. If the process died while waiting
          for the agent, the work record survived, but the handoff state did
          not.
        </p>
        <p>
          The duplicate is not a mystery once you see the recovery path's
          view. After the kill, it sees claimed work with no recorded session
          and starts a replacement. The original agent is still running, so
          both agents complete, and the older attempt finishes last,
          overwriting the current receipt.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="tco__player">
        <div id="before-temporal-player" data-cast-src={beforeCast}></div>
        <p class="tco__player-note" hidden></p>
        <figcaption>
          The right panes are where it goes wrong: two agent processes for
          one work item, then a stale receipt over the current one.
          <a href={`${root}/demo/before-temporal.mp4`} download="before-temporal.mp4">Download the video</a>,
          or the <a href={beforeCast} download="before-temporal.cast">raw asciinema event log</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h3 class="tco__demo-subhead">With Temporal: the Worker dies, the agent does not</h3>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>What to watch, per kill:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            After a checkpoint: the replacement Worker resumes from heartbeat
            details, and session creations stay at one.
          </li>
          <li>
            Before any checkpoint: the resolver is called a second time and
            returns the existing session instead of creating another one.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The second kill is the decisive one. Surviving a kill after a
          checkpoint does not by itself prove duplicate-launch prevention;
          the invariant report draws the same distinction.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="tco__player">
        <div id="worker-kill-player" data-cast-src={cast}></div>
        <p class="tco__player-note" hidden></p>
        <figcaption>
          The full recording keeps real timing, including the pause where
          Temporal notices the dead <TermTip
            term="worker"
            tip="The process that polls Temporal's task queues and executes Workflow and Activity code. It is the one process this design allows to die."
          >Worker</TermTip>.
          <a href={`${root}/demo/worker-kill.mp4`} download="worker-kill.mp4">Download the video</a>,
          or the <a href={cast} download="worker-kill.cast">raw asciinema event log</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          Both kills end the same way: one agent session and one fenced
          receipt.
        </p>
        <p>
          The demo runs on one host against a local dev server, with a
          file-backed store and a fixture process in place of a coding agent.
          It proves the boundary, on real Temporal, with a real
          operating-system kill signal. The before-state arm drives the same
          fixture agent over the same protocol; only the orchestrator
          differs.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={`${evidence}/worker-kill/verify-report.txt`}>The invariant report, both arms</a></li>
          <li><a href={`${evidence}/provenance.json`}>Run provenance</a></li>
          <li><a href={`${root}/article#the-evidence-and-where-to-go-deeper`}>All run evidence, in the article appendix</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section tco__numbered">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Section 03</p>
        <h2>What is actually running</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>Each path carries its own status and its own evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <table class="tco__table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Path</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Evidence</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Result delivery and exact acknowledgement</td>
            <td>Production</td>
            <td>Exact receipts, redelivery until acknowledged, independent watchdog</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Temporal-controlled claim and agent launch</td>
            <td>Shadow; bounded canary completed</td>
            <td>One claim, one session, one fenced receipt; the agent side was a scripted stand-in</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Worker recovery on one host</td>
            <td>Demonstrated</td>
            <td>Recorded harness run; both kill positions end with one session</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cross-host recovery</td>
            <td>Not demonstrated</td>
            <td>Single-host fixture only</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          The same pattern now runs continuously for result delivery. A
          finished outcome remains owed until the coordinating agent
          acknowledges the exact result generation and agent session. Delivery
          may repeat; the identity of the result does not. The delivery path,
          step by step, is in
          <a href={`${root}/article#whats-running-now`}>the article</a>, and
          what the evidence shows so far, including the measured
          infrastructure cost, is in
          <a href={`${root}/article#what-the-evidence-shows-so-far`}>its numbers section</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section tco__numbered">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Section 04</p>
        <h2>What it did not fix, and what it cost</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="tco__copy prose">
        <p>
          Temporal gives the procedure a durable owner. It does not make
          external effects exactly once. A Worker can still die after an
          external call succeeds but before Activity completion is recorded,
          causing the Activity to run again. Making that retry safe remains
          application work.
        </p>
        <p>
          One canary completed the agent work correctly but placed the wrong
          store identity in the outcome envelope. Temporal then retried that
          invalid envelope every fifteen minutes, exactly as instructed.
          Durability preserved the application mistake rather than correcting
          it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Durability also adds real engineering cost. Contributors now need to
          understand determinism, replay compatibility, fencing, idempotency,
          and Workflow versioning before changing the orchestration safely.
          The deployment gains a Temporal server and Worker, but the larger
          cost is the additional reasoning the system requires.
        </p>
        <p>
          Not every job earns that complexity. One maintenance task performs
          forty-four seconds of synchronous work every two hours. If it fails,
          the next scheduled run can safely try again, so it remains cron plus
          a lock.
        </p>
        <p>
          The useful dividing line is what a crash leaves unresolved. If the
          application's own durable state can tell the next process exactly
          what to do, ordinary retry machinery may be enough. If the crash
          leaves the system unable to say whether an operation started, which
          attempt owns the result, or whether the result was acknowledged, the
          procedure needs a durable owner.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tco__section">
      <header class="tco__section-head">
        <p class="kicker">Go deeper</p>
        <h2>The rest of the case study</h2>
      </header>
      <nav class="tco__tabs" aria-label="Complete case study materials">
        <a href={`${root}/article`}><span>01</span> Read the complete article</a>
        <a href={`${root}/code`}><span>02</span> Inspect annotated source</a>
        <a href={`${root}/article#the-evidence-and-where-to-go-deeper`}><span>03</span> Browse the run evidence</a>
        <a href={`${evidence}/provenance.json`}><span>04</span> View provenance</a>
      </nav>
    </section>
  </article>
</PageLayout>

<script>
  import * as AsciinemaPlayer from "asciinema-player";

  // Both casts are 120x38 tmux sessions. Posters sit on the finished report
  // rather than an early beat: the opening frames are a few lines over an
  // empty terminal, which reads as a large black void before anyone presses
  // play. Marker times are re-measured from each recording's event stream.
  // `notes` maps a marker index to a status line shown under the player from
  // that marker until the next one fires; the recordings keep real timing,
  // and the heartbeat-timeout pause reads as a stall without one.
  const players: {
    id: string;
    poster: string;
    markers: [number, string][];
    notes: Record<number, string>;
  }[] = [
    {
      id: "before-temporal-player",
      poster: "npt:30.5",
      markers: [
        [2.1, "The orchestrator claims; the agent starts"],
        [4.6, "kill -9: the orchestrator is gone"],
        [7.6, "Restart, with an empty memory"],
        [12.2, "A second agent for the same task"],
        [23.7, "The stale receipt lands on top"],
      ],
      notes: {
        1: "The orchestrator is dead. The agent it launched is still running.",
        2: "The restarted orchestrator sees claimed work with no recorded session, so it starts a replacement.",
      },
    },
    {
      id: "worker-kill-player",
      poster: "npt:48.0",
      markers: [
        [3.5, "Arm 1 starts"],
        [8.4, "kill -9: Worker one is gone"],
        [26.6, "Temporal retries; workflow completed"],
        [27.7, "Arm 2 starts"],
        [31.0, "kill -9 before any checkpoint"],
        [41.1, "Retry re-resolves; workflow completed"],
      ],
      notes: {
        1: "The Worker is dead. The agent is still running. Temporal is waiting out the heartbeat timeout before it retries.",
        4: "The second Worker is dead before any checkpoint exists. The retry has to ask the resolver again.",
      },
    },
  ];
  for (const { id, poster, markers, notes } of players) {
    const mountPoint = document.getElementById(id);
    const src = mountPoint?.dataset.castSrc;
    if (mountPoint && src) {
      const player = AsciinemaPlayer.create(src, mountPoint, {
        cols: 120,
        rows: 38,
        preload: true,
        poster,
        fit: "width",
        markers,
      });
      const note = mountPoint
        .closest("figure")
        ?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".tco__player-note");
      if (note) {
        player.addEventListener("marker", ({ index }: { index: number }) => {
          const text = notes[index];
          if (text) {
            note.textContent = text;
            note.hidden = false;
          } else {
            note.hidden = true;
          }
        });
        player.addEventListener("ended", () => {
          note.hidden = true;
        });
      }
    }
  }
</script>

<style>
  .tco {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-3xl);
  }

